# Visualizer — force-directed graph UI + VS Code extension.

import "code_graph.elv"

c visualizer {
    d: "Force-directed graph UI + VS Code extension; watch mode re-analyzes on save. Elevator kinds render with hierarchy-encoded node sizes."
    cr: "ui/", "vscode-extension/"
    f visual_scopes
    f aggregation
    f quality_report
    f saved_views
    f window_mirror
    f diff_reading
    f engine_endpoint
    f theming
    f node_encoding
    f edge_encoding
    f grouping
    f viewport_overview
    f entity_search
    f view_search
    f entity_reading
    f editor_sync
    f spec_pairing
    f keyboard_layer
}

f engine_endpoint {
    d: "Which engine the UI talks to, resolved at runtime rather than assumed to be whoever served the page: the VS Code webview's apiBase first, then ?api=, then localStorage, then same-origin. apiUrl composes that base with the serve-mode slug prefix and the pairing token; flatApiUrl takes the base but never the slug, for the endpoints that live outside the per-repo namespace. connection.ts probes /api/hello before anything loads, which is what lets the connect screen tell a refused origin from a dead port from a server that is not mezz — three failures a browser reports identically. liveReload runs the same probe when the SSE stream drops, so a broken stream reads as not-live instead of nothing-is-changing."
    cr: "ui/src/endpoint.ts", "ui/src/stores/connection.ts", "ui/src/components/ConnectScreen.svelte"
}

f visual_scopes {
    d: "File-path scope filtering for the graph canvas (filterToSelection), and the two ways a reader moves between scopes without losing their place. Elevator entities close over their transitive Contains descendants and attached Concepts across .elv file boundaries — a Category never renders without the Features it declares elsewhere; code entities keep strict file scoping. Descendants only, so narrowing to one branch still narrows. Narrowing used to be one-way: every door into a new picture replaced the old one with no way back, which made the rational move not to drill at all. Back and up are different questions and both are answered here — retrace walks the sequence of pictures, address walks the folder tree — while extendScopeToParents stays the one gesture that widens a scope with no single address."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/scope.ts"
    fu marked_set
    fu retrace
    fu address
    fu neighbourhood
    fu shape
}

fu f.visual_scopes.shape {
    d: "The third canvas mode, and the first that draws the graph f.metrics.shape SCORES. ADR 0012's whole premise is that the number is a claim about a picture somebody looks at, and until this nobody could: the force graph shows every file at once, which past a few hundred nodes is the hairball the measure exists to describe, and tree mode gives a readable picture while flattening every relationship to the same weight — so WHICH edge is the violation, the one question a shape verdict raises, was exactly what neither could say. viewMode gains a third value and the whole of tree mode's machinery is reused: displayPlan fills the same treePositions map, GraphView pins to it, stops the simulation and auto-fits, because a shape is laid out and not simulated. What makes it its own mode rather than a second source of tree positions is meaning — a tree is a reach around a selection, a shape is a claim about one folder — and that the edges carry a verdict here. The picture is FETCHED (stores/shape.ts, GET /api/shape) and never derived from graphData: levels, loops and doors were decided by the pass that produced the verdict, and re-deriving them in the browser would be a second answer free to draw a picture the Quality panel denies. shapePicture lives in stores/graph.ts beside ringFocusPath rather than with the fetching, because graphData derives from it and the reverse import would close the store cycle collapseGraph's header warns about; viewMode had to move ABOVE graphData in that file for the same reason, a const read before its declaration being a TDZ throw rather than an undefined. shapeResolvers is what puts it on the canvas: a GrainOf plus the new ScopeOf, since a file three directories down has to collapse into the immediate CHILD of the folder holding it and that is an ancestor moduleOf never names. Answering null for everything else is the filtering half — an aggregation, not a filter stage, which is why the strip does not list it and why displayPlan returns before the kind, language and file filters run at all: they have nothing to say about nine circles the reader asked for by name, and a File unticked in some earlier scope would otherwise empty the view. Only edges the picture has a reading for are drawn: measured on src/mcp, 43 outsider-to-outsider lines against 31 that carry a verdict, and left in they would be most of the drawing in a colour meaning nothing. The way in is the Quality panel's Shape cell — number to picture in one click, which is the path ADR 0012 was built for — and the way out is a chip in the toolbar, the only thing on screen that says which folder is being drawn. Known limit: the picture counts doors over every file the ENGINE analysed while the canvas draws only what the reader's scope loaded, so a narrowed scope thins the gutters without changing the verdict."
    references: f.metrics, f.quality_report, f.edge_encoding
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/shapeView.ts", "ui/src/stores/shape.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/collapseGraph.ts", "ui/scripts/shape-view.test.ts"
}

fu f.visual_scopes.neighbourhood {
    d: "The second closure, and the one the file boundary forces: a Markdown note IS its file, so every relationship it has crosses that boundary and strict file scoping cuts all of them at once. Scope to one .md — which f.editor_sync does on every editor switch — and the canvas drew a single circle while f.entity_reading.relate rendered no Relationships section at all, there being nothing left to list; indistinguishable from a document nobody links to. noteLinkNeighbours closes the scope over the unit that carries the meaning instead: the note, what it links to, and what links to it. ONE hop and notes at both ends (isNote reads the parser's `markdown` tag, the same predicate isSpecEntity takes) — two hops off a hub document is most of a doc corpus, and the reader who wants the corpus can select it. Measured on this repo: CLAUDE.md 1 → 8 nodes, docs/adr 11 → 39, a 289-note folder 289 → 321, so it stays a narrowing. An unresolved link target comes along, Obsidian's ghost note being a node the reader is meant to see; a code ghost, which has no file at all, does not. Pure and store-free so the one-hop bound is asserted rather than clicked."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/noteScope.ts", "ui/scripts/note-scope.test.ts"
}

fu f.visual_scopes.marked_set {
    d: "How a reader gets from 'which files talk to each other' to 'which entities in THESE files talk to each other'. ⌘/Ctrl-click marks a node, `m` marks the hovered one, and drillIntoMarks spends the whole set through setScopes — the plural is the point, because a relationship runs BETWEEN files, so drilling into one end throws away the other and drilling into the folder holding both usually brings back too much to draw at entity level. Nothing in it names an aggregation level: autoLevel re-picks from RENDER_BUDGET against the new, smaller scope, which is the same mechanism that already opens any small scope at Entity level. markedStats runs that comparison against the index BEFORE the click, so a set too big to draw as entities says 'opens at file level' instead of silently returning another file view. Marks are PATHS, never ids, and here that is load-bearing rather than convenient: collapseGraph mints fresh ids on every level change, and a mark's whole job is to survive the level change it causes. markPathOf reads file_path alone and never kind_raw — a File rollup's file_path is its own path, a Module rollup's is its directory, an entity's is the file it came from — so one field means 'the narrowest scope this circle is evidence of' at every level, and the ring lands on the file circle and on every entity inside it for the same reason. Ghosts and the root Module are unmarkable, both spelling their path '' , which as a scope is the whole repo. A mark is not a filter and changes nothing on screen but its own ring: comparing two files means keeping the neighbourhood that made them interesting. The set is cleared once the scope applies — every node then drawn sits under a marked path, so keeping it would ring the entire canvas, and the scope tree is the durable record."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/markSet.ts", "ui/src/stores/marks.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte", "ui/src/components/CanvasToolbar.svelte"
}

fu f.visual_scopes.retrace {
    d: "Back and forward over the pictures the canvas has drawn — six gestures replace it (a collapsed node, a region, the marked set, a committed search, a file focused from VS Code, a restored view) and none could be undone. A frame is a ViewState, the record f.saved_views already defined, so there is no second notion of a picture: record/stepBack/stepForward in viewmodels/viewHistory.ts are two arrays over that codec, and stores/viewHistory.ts wires them to captureState and restoreState. What is new is the rule for when a frame is banked. scope.ts carries a registered hook — onBeforeNavigate, registered rather than imported because the history needs savedViews which needs scope, and that cycle hands the other module undefined while its top-level derived is being built. asNavigation wraps the gestures that REPLACE the picture (setScopes, addScopes, drillIn, drillIntoMarks, focusScope, extendScopeToParents, clearScope, selectAllScope, restoreState) and toggleScope is deliberately outside it: the scope tree's checkbox adjusts a picture, and five ticks then a drill must be one step back to the five-folder reading rather than five. Its depth guard captures at the OUTERMOST gesture, or drillIn — which sets autoLevel before calling setScopes — would bank the flag it was meant to remember; withoutNavigation is the mirror, and is what stops a back press recording the step it is undoing. worthRecording drops a frame with no include rules, because the first navigation of every session replaces an empty canvas and a stack bottoming out on a blank screen makes the last back press a punishment. Capped at HISTORY_LIMIT 50 and never persisted: a saved view is a reading, a history is where the reader is standing, and UI-082 already put position on the far side of that line. `[` and `]` are global bindings, since a scope is committed from the sidebar and a region focused from the canvas."
    references: f.saved_views
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/viewHistory.ts", "ui/src/stores/viewHistory.ts", "ui/src/components/CanvasToolbar.svelte", "ui/scripts/view-history.test.ts"
}

fu f.visual_scopes.address {
    d: "Where the reader is, as climbable crumbs beside the back/forward arrows — repo › ui › src › viewmodels, every ancestor one click. Deliberately NOT a breadcrumb of the history: a back stack of a folder, a committed search and a marked set has no ancestry in it, and drawing those as a › b › c promises a containment that is not there. Two axes, two controls. scopeAddress turns the ADR 0009 rule list into one of three shapes, because a scope is not always a place: empty (no include rules — drawn as nothing, since a bare `repo` over a blank canvas claims the opposite of the truth), path (exactly one plain prefix, climbable), opaque (several paths or a glob — named for what it is, `2 paths`, and offered no climb, because showing the first of six marked paths would be a confident wrong answer). An exclusion leaves the address alone and raises `filtered` instead: ui/src minus its tests is still ui/src, but the strip must not claim the folder is shown whole. elideCrumbs cuts from the LEFT past MAX_CRUMBS and always keeps the root — `… › viewmodels › savedViews` still answers where am I, `repo › ui › src › …` does not. Each crumb goes through drillIn rather than a bespoke scope write, which is what makes the climb undoable and re-enables autoLevel, so jumping two levels opens the ancestor at the detail it can afford. The crumb you are standing on is a span, not a disabled button: there is nowhere for it to go."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/scopeCrumbs.ts", "ui/src/components/ScopeCrumbs.svelte", "ui/scripts/scope-crumbs.test.ts"
}

f aggregation {
    d: "One node per entity, per file, or per folder — the same graph read at three grains, and the module grain is nothing but dirname(file_path), in every language mezz parses. collapseGraph is the whole of it and returns the same GraphData shape, so the display plan, the filters and the selection flow downstream cannot tell a rollup from an entity. The level is no longer a property of the picture: collapseGraph takes an optional GrainOf, a function from a node to the grain it draws at, and grainFromLevel recovers the uniform case exactly — the fast path that returns raw untouched survives only when no resolver is passed, since the caller and not this module is the one that knows whether a plan answers Entity everywhere. scopeIdFor answers entity grain BEFORE the file_path guard, a ghost being a circle at Entity level and having nowhere to go only below it; grainFromLevel keeps the guard on its own side, because the root module's path and a ghost's file_path are both the empty string and an expanded root would otherwise promote every external symbol in the repo onto the canvas. Cross-scope edges merge into one DependsOn per ordered pair carrying a per-kind breakdown, because 'file A calls file B' is not a sentence; an edge with entities at BOTH ends keeps its own kind, which is what makes an expanded view mixed rather than uniform. Expansion opens exactly one level and is held as PATHS, never ids: collapseGraph mints fresh ids on every level change, so an id-keyed expansion stops matching the moment the view moves — the same reason marks are paths. scopeIdFor answers 'which scope' and may answer NONE, which is only ever a ghost: an external reference has no file, and 6 598 of them against 13 615 real entities cannot each be a circle at a collapsed level. That `null` used to be the empty string — the same key moduleOf gives the repo ROOT — and the edge merge dropped both, testing `!srcScope` where it meant 'no scope at all', so every relationship of every root-level file vanished at module level and the ghosts drew as one bogus '(root)' rollup at file level. In a doc graph the root is where the hub documents live, so the reader who selected it got a Details pane with no Relationships section and no way to tell that from a file that depends on nothing. It must stay store-free, since stores/graph.ts imports it and any store import here closes a cycle that leaves graphData undefined at module-eval time. Rollup metrics come from the engine's files and modules arrays rather than being recomputed, and composite_score is left undefined when the backend omits it, 0 being the BEST score and therefore the wrong default to paint unknown with."
    references: f.visual_scopes, f.node_encoding, f.saved_views
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/collapseGraph.ts", "ui/src/stores/graph.ts"
    fu redundancy
    fu rings
}

fu f.aggregation.rings {
    d: "Spending the render budget where the reader is looking instead of uniformly. RENDER_BUDGET is 400 and pickLevel spends it all-or-nothing — 401 heavy entities in scope and the whole canvas drops to File, including the eight the reader came for — so the question a graph is best at, where does this sit in the system, was the one the level control made most expensive to ask. planRingGrain answers it with a grain per hop: hopDistances walks the raw entity graph UNDIRECTED from a seed, because a function that calls the focus is exactly as near as one the focus calls and direction is already the direction filters' question. ringsFor(reach, outer) is the two controls composed — reach+1 entity rings then the level button's grain — with deliberately no graded file ring between, a ring the reader cannot see in either control being worth less than the promise that the level button says what the rest of the graph is. The focus is a PATH and never an id, for the reason marks are: seedFromPath resolves it to entity ids, so a reader can focus a File or Module rollup, and seeding from selectedNode instead would make graphData depend on the selection and delete the node that seeded the plan the moment it drew. A scope is atomic and that is the whole difficulty — rings are drawn over a graph, a picture over a tree, and a file can hold one entity the focus calls and another nothing reaches; promoteScopes lifts each scope to the finest grain any member asked for and clampToScope then reads it back, so a far file inside a reached module draws as one File and never as a second Module overlapping the File beside it. drawnCount counts distinct scopes rather than nodes, so what a ring costs is known before it is drawn. Measured on ui/src/viewmodels: 38 File circles become 163 entities beside 35 File rollups, and reach 3 is 1 771 entities — the budget is not enforced here yet, pickLevel still choosing the outer grain blind to the plan. Known gap: remapToContainingScope remaps a vanished selection only COARSER, every level change before this one having escalated, so turning the focus on opens the selected File and drops the selection where the level buttons keep it."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/mixedGrain.ts", "ui/src/stores/graph.ts", "ui/src/components/CanvasToolbar.svelte", "ui/scripts/mixed-grain.test.ts"
    references: f.visual_scopes
}

fu f.aggregation.redundancy {
    d: "Saying when a level would redraw the picture already on screen. In a document graph the Markdown parser emits one Note per file — a link addresses a whole document, so headings are not entities — and collapsing entities into files therefore removes nothing: Entity and File are the same canvas, and a reader who pressed Entity, watched nothing move, and pressed it again learned only that a control looked broken. everyFileIsOneEntity answers it from the pre-collapse nodes alone, distinct file_path against node count, and never asks what language anything is written in: a future document language inherits the answer, and a filtered code view that happens to be one entity per file gets it too, which is correct, because Entity really would redraw File there. Asking a collapsed graph would be meaningless — every file holds one node at File level by construction — so it takes rawEntityGraph. The button stays ENABLED and is dimmed instead: Entity level is the baseline the expansion set is expressed against, and a control the reader cannot come back through is worse than one that repeats itself. The dimming drops when it is the active level, the level you are looking at not being allowed to be the faintest thing in the group. An empty graph is deliberately not the redundant shape, the blank canvas having its own message. All three buttons carry the plain definition on hover, which is where a reader asking what a module is now finds out."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/collapseGraph.ts", "ui/src/components/CanvasToolbar.svelte"
}

f quality_report {
    d: "The Quality panel: a composite score per entity, the ranked entity table, and the file and module rollups behind its Files and Modules tabs. Everything in it is governed by one population — the set of entities the numbers describe — chosen by the reader through qualityAnalysisScope and by nothing else. analysisGraph is where that choice becomes a graph, and every downstream store (qualityRows, qualitySummary, repoQuality, rowsByScope, fileRows, moduleRows) derives from it rather than from graphData, so no two figures in the panel can be counting different entities. The population is deliberately not the canvas's picture: the Files and Folders control filters what is drawn and leaves the numbers alone (ADR 0010), so a scoped canvas sitting beside a repo-wide figure is the normal state and not an error — which is exactly why every figure has to say which population it is on."
    references: f.visual_scopes, f.editor_sync
    cr: "ui/src/components/QualityReport.svelte", "ui/src/stores/quality.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/qualityPopulation.ts"
    fu pick
    fu aggregate
    fu withhold
    fu explain
}

fu f.quality_report.pick {
    d: "Which entities the numbers cover. POPULATIONS is the one table of six — the whole analysis scope (the default), the scope tree selection, what the canvas is drawing, the current selection, the file open in the editor, and the files a diff changed — and it carries both the long label and the short one, so the banner and the summary header cannot name different populations over the same numbers. analysisGraph falls back to the whole scope when the signal a population needs is absent; right at runtime and wrong to leave unsaid, so the picker disables such a population rather than naming one and showing another. selectionPopulation makes a selected entity stand for what it CONTAINS and not just itself, or selecting a struct would report a single row and call it a scope. narrowRollups then cuts the file and module rows down to the population's paths WITHOUT recomputing their numbers: the engine computes cohesion and fan-out over the whole file, and cohesion over half a file is not a lower cohesion, it is nothing. Kept pure and store-free so scopeHolds, selectionPopulation and changedFilePaths can be unit-tested (npm run test:quality-population) apart from displayPlan and the VS Code adapter."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/qualityPopulation.ts"
}

fu f.quality_report.aggregate {
    d: "One number over a set of rows, and what that number is allowed to be called. aggregateRows is LOC-weighted rather than a plain mean — small healthy getters would otherwise mask the large complex function beside them — and it is the single body behind both the banner (repoQuality) and the per-scope aggregate column (scopeRowsFrom), so a rollup cannot disagree with the summary above it. SCORE_TIERS holds the ok/warn cutoffs once, and tierFromScore is the only reading of them. The banner's label is derived from the same POPULATIONS entry that names the population lower down, because it read 'repo score' unconditionally while the value beneath it already moved with the picker: narrowing to the current selection relabelled nothing and changed everything."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/quality.ts", "ui/src/components/QualityReport.svelte"
}

fu f.quality_report.withhold {
    d: "Saying that a number was not measured, rather than printing the number that means it was fine. Scope coupling is counted over dependency edges only, so in a document graph every scope reports fan_in 0 and fan_out 0 while the canvas plainly draws arrows between the folders — and 0 is the flattering end of that scale, so an unmeasured folder reads as a decoupled one, which is the reading a reader is least likely to double-check. couplingCell is the single answer to what one of those cells should say: it takes the measured count beside the ref_fan_in / ref_fan_out the engine now reports, and returns an em dash plus the reason only when the count is zero AND references exist. Both conditions matter — a scope with real coupling shows its real number even when references also run through it, because the count is then a measurement and annotating it would be noise, and a scope with neither keeps its 0, which is earned and must stay distinguishable. One function, three surfaces: the detail panel's two metric chips, the Quality table's Fin and Fout cells, and the row scopeRowValues copies out, so a pasted row cannot claim a zero the panel refused to. It renders through the existing metric-na grey rather than a new state, and follows cohesion, which was already Option and already read as unmeasured through tierCohesion — the counts were the half with no escape hatch, 0 being a legal count. Same rule node_encoding applies when it withholds a metric with no rollup at the current level instead of letting it read zero."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/scopeCoupling.ts", "ui/src/components/QualityReport.svelte", "ui/src/components/EntityInfo.svelte", "ui/src/stores/quality.ts"
    references: f.metrics, f.entity_reading, f.node_encoding
}

fu f.quality_report.explain {
    d: "Printing the reason a row is where it is, not just the verdict — the Shape column, which is the panel's one reading of f.metrics.shape. shapeCell composes the cell and shapeBlockerLabel turns the engine's tagged blocker into a phrase short enough to sit in it, so a row reads 'hierarchical · many doors · entry 49%' and the reader is told which of four gates to go and fix. The blend behind it stays in the title tooltip: compliance is a weighted mean, two folders at 72% can need opposite fixes, and a number you cannot act on has no claim on the cell. The blocker is NOT re-derived here — the thresholds are configurable and live in Thresholds, so a second copy of the comparisons in the panel would be a second answer free to disagree with the tier printed beside it; the panel only formats what classify already decided. Its labels drop the words the tier already says (cyclic and tangled show the bare measurement, the tiers above them do not name their own gate and keep the wording). SHAPE_HINTS mirrors ShapePattern::hint() in prose rather than shipping it per row, for the reason SMELL_META does. The whole column is behind mode === 'modules' — a file has no children so it draws no graph and carries no shape — which is also why the 'shape below hierarchical' severity option is only offered there and answers true in Files mode rather than emptying the table. SHAPE_RANK spells the ordering out instead of sorting on a raw number, higher being better here and the reverse of every other column in the table; shapeOrder ranks by tier with compliance as the tie-break inside it. The tally line under the heading is a ladder read worst-first, and it inherits the engine's single-child waiver — a folder holding one file counts as fractal in it. SHAPE_COPY_HEADER gives the modules table five columns the files one does not have rather than five empty cells."
    cr: "ui/src/components/QualityReport.svelte", "ui/src/stores/quality.ts", "ui/src/types/graph.ts"
    references: f.metrics
}

f saved_views {
    d: "Naming the picture on the canvas so a reader can come back to it, and switch between two of them to see the same code at two levels or two filters. What a view holds is one line: everything that decides WHICH entities and relationships reach the canvas — the scope rules, the level and autoLevel, the ring focus and its reach, the kind, direction, language and file filters, the per-level tri-states, the ghost toggles, the spec cross-filter and a committed search — and nothing that decides where the reader is standing. The ring focus earns its place on that side rather than sitting with folderCohesion as a setting: once it is set the level names only the OUTER grain, so a view restoring the level without it would draw a picture the reader never saw, and stateSummary prints it for the same reason. Camera, selection, expanded scopes, open panes and the Quality population are all out: restoring must not move the reader's cursor, and a view that reopened a pane would be doing two jobs. Restoring writes the same stores the controls write, so there is no view mode and a restored view stays editable — which makes the order load-bearing, since applySelection republishes and seedFromGraph re-seeds the kind filters to 'everything present', so anything the seed touches is written after the scope and anything it deliberately leaves alone (the exclusions) either side. Staleness is expected rather than exceptional: hidden files, committed search ids and spec ids are pruned against three different universes — the full graph, the graph at the level now being drawn, and the spec graph — and the count is reported instead of the restore failing. Scope rules are never pruned, a path that exists on another branch being a reason to save a view rather than a defect in one. activeViewId is derived from the stores rather than set by the restore, so it goes null the moment the reader changes anything, which is the honest signal and what makes Update mean something. The codec is not private to this feature: restoreState takes a bare ViewState and restoreView is only the named door, because f.visual_scopes' history frames are saved views nobody named."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/savedViews.ts", "ui/src/stores/savedViews.ts", "ui/src/components/SavedViewsSection.svelte"
    cr.server: "src/server/views_handler.rs"
    fu store
}

fu f.saved_views.store {
    d: "Where a saved view lives, and what each failure means. <root>/.mezz/views.json through GET/PUT /api/views, repo-scope by ADR 0008's line — a view is a set of paths, languages and kinds of this repo, true whoever clones it, and worth committing beside the code it describes; it also puts the browser UI and the VS Code webview on one list, both talking HTTP to the same mezz watch. mezz serve never gets the routes, its tree having arrived from a URL a stranger pasted, so the UI reads a 404 as 'this server has no view store' and falls back to localStorage keyed by the serve slug — never by the analyzed root, which is fetched by a component mounting after the load and would have the list read under one key and written under another. A 5xx is the opposite conclusion: the file is there and could not be parsed, so saving is refused rather than replacing a list nobody has seen. The envelope is typed and the view is not — id, name and saved_at so the file diffs as a named list and the server can refuse a nameless entry, state opaque so capturing one more toggle stays a UI-only change. Writes are whole-list and atomic (temp file plus rename), and the client rolls its list back when one fails, a list showing a view the file does not have being how a reader loses one without being told."
    cr: "src/server/views_handler.rs", "ui/src/stores/savedViews.ts"
}

f window_mirror {
    d: "Two windows of the app kept on one reading, because two monitors hold panes one window never can. What syncs is the reading — a ViewState, the codec f.saved_views already defined, so there is no second notion of a picture — plus the Attention that codec deliberately excludes, which for a mirror is most of the value. What does not sync is the layout: pane widths and zoom stay local, or the second window is a duplicate rather than an extension, which is the opposite of the point. whatToApply is three-way and not a boolean — none for an echo or for the picture already on screen, attention for a reader who moved within the same reading, all for a reading that moved — because restoreState republishes the scope and waits on a graph fetch, while pointing at nodes is the commonest gesture there is; a window that ran the full restore every time its peer's pointer crossed a node would spend the session refetching a graph it already had. withoutNavigation wraps the restore for the reason f.visual_scopes.retrace gives: without it every change made in the other window lands in this one's back stack and the two windows record each other's steps. The applying flag is not what terminates the echo — whatToApply already answers none for a state the peer holds — it is what saves the round of traffic and stops a slow restoreState racing its own echo. BroadcastChannel is the whole delivery mechanism, so: same browser, same profile, same machine. Two machines, or Chrome talking to Firefox, would need a relay through the watch server and is a different feature; the peer count exists so that limit is visible rather than discovered, a mirror with nobody on the other end saying so. channelName is keyed by repo, mezz serve hosting many from one origin and two windows on different projects being two unrelated sessions. normalizeMessage is total over garbage for the reason normalizeViews is — a tab left open across a deploy is the realistic case, and the answer to a payload we cannot read is to ignore it, not to throw inside an event handler where nothing will catch it. mirrorOn is persisted because opening the second window IS the gesture, and it should come up already listening."
    references: f.saved_views, f.entity_reading
    cr: "ui/src/stores/mirror.ts", "ui/scripts/mirror.test.ts"
    fu attention
    fu settle
}

fu f.window_mirror.attention {
    d: "What travels beside the reading: the selected entity, the entity under the pointer, whether the preview is frozen, the view mode, and the spec pane's open path. All of it applies without a refetch, which is why it is Attention and not ViewState. hovered is here because of what reads it — f.entity_reading.describe prefers hover over selection, so a window whose panes are the whole reason the second monitor exists shows nothing at all while the pointer moves on the other screen; UI-095 first filed hover with the layout beside pane widths and zoom, which was wrong for exactly the setup the feature is for. specPath is here because of the line f.saved_views draws rather than in spite of it: a saved view records what reaches the canvas, and a drill path decides what the SPEC pane draws, while the thing that does reach the canvas is the spec selection, already carried as ViewState.spec. Filing it here also makes drilling an attention change rather than an all, so opening a Category on one screen does not cost a graph refetch on the other. It compares as a sequence and not as a set — unlike every filter in ViewState, the same two entities in the other order are a different descent. An id resolves against BOTH the code graph and the spec graph, because f.spec_pairing draws two subgraphs of one analysis and only one of them is in graphData: that store is the scoped graph collapsed to the level now being drawn, so a spec entity resolved there only when its .elv happened to be in scope AND the level happened to be entity, and when it did not the peer answered a mirrored spec selection by clearing its own and blanking its Details column. The spec graph is read unfiltered by followAnalysisScope, that toggle being per-window layout like the pane widths — a window with it on must still be able to name what its peer selected."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/mirror.ts"
}

fu f.window_mirror.settle {
    d: "Whose pointer wins when both windows have one. Local wins: two readers sweeping two canvases would otherwise overwrite each other every few pixels and neither pane would settle on anything, and the one whose pointer is moving is the one asking a question. hoveredNode alone cannot say whether what is under it arrived from this window's pointer or the other's, so hoverToShow is told the remote hover this window last adopted, and that is what makes the rule decidable at all; once the local pointer leaves, the peer's is picked back up rather than the pane falling to the selection, or a mirror stops mirroring the moment you touch it and stays stopped. The third input is a hover a local CLICK refused, and it is the one the two-window setup made visible: in the arrangement this feature exists for — canvas on one screen, panes on the other — the pane window has no pointer on the graph at all, so it is always showing the peer's, and selecting in its own Spec pane moved Details while Description went on narrating the peer's node. Nothing about local, remote or adopted separates that from an ordinary remote hover. The refusal is on that one entity and never on mirroring: the moment the peer's pointer moves it is a new gesture and is adopted again. Clearing a selection deliberately does not refuse anything — unpinning asks to follow a pointer again, and the nearest pointer such a window has is the peer's. Reconciling also runs when this window's own hover clears, because 'the local pointer left, so the peer's applies again' is a transition no message announces."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/mirror.ts", "ui/src/stores/mirror.ts"
}

f node_encoding {
    d: "What a circle on the canvas means: size carries a magnitude metric (SIZE_CHANNELS, default loc), fill carries severity from the same composite_score the Quality panel ranks by. buildNodeEncoding is the one place that answers 'how big, what colour' — GraphView has two node-join sites and the legend a third, and each carrying its own copy is how the theme bug happened. Area, not radius, reads as magnitude, so the default curve is sqrt over R_MIN..R_MAX against a domain taken from the nodes in play, not a global maximum — but both halves of that mapping are the reader's to choose now (fu shape), the curve and the width of the range, and radiusRange is the one place the drawn range is derived. Two things are deliberately not the bottom of a ramp: a node with no metrics renders in half-opacity grey (hollow, not 'zero') at a radius held as a FRACTION of the span rather than the flat 9px it was, or at a wide scale 'unknown' would land back on the floor and read as 'smallest', and a graph whose nodes mostly carry no metrics — isMetricFreeGraph, a majority test over isMetricFreeNode, an Elevator domain graph being the usual case — drops to kindRadius and the kind palette, because domain kinds carry no code metrics at all. A metric with no rollup at the current aggregation level is withheld from the channel picker rather than silently reading zero. requiresDiff extends that rule along a second axis: a channel can also depend on a comparison existing, which is how Lines changed is offered in diff mode and nowhere else — sizeChannelsFor drops it from the picker and buildNodeEncoding refuses it independently, because a channel is persisted in localStorage and restored from a saved view, so the picker withholding it is not enough. Every channel but that one is read off the node; churn and degree arrive through SizeContext, one being a property of the drawn graph and the other of the comparison, and encoding.ts passes diffChurnAt only while diffChurnAvailable, so the picker and the encoding cannot disagree about whether the canvas can say it."
    references: f.metrics, f.diff_reading
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/nodeEncoding.ts", "ui/src/stores/encoding.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte"
    fu legend
    fu shape
}

fu f.node_encoding.shape {
    d: "How the chosen metric reaches the radius, once WHICH metric is no longer the only question. SIZE_CURVES is five monotone maps of [0,1] onto [0,1] — area (sqrt, the default and the pre-existing behaviour), linear, log (log10(1+99u)/2), exponential ((e^4u-1)/(e^4-1)) and a logistic s-curve — and every one of them pins f(0)=0 and f(1)=1, which is the contract that lets the choice be free: only the distribution between smallest and largest moves, so the size scale cannot change meaning when the curve does and no curve can draw a small node bigger than a large one. Each carries its own invert, because the legend samples the ramp at fixed fractions of the RADIUS range and has to print the VALUES those radii stand for — with the curve fixed at sqrt legendStops could hardcode that answer (0.04 and 0.400 of vMax), and holding f and its inverse together is also what makes the pair testable as a round-trip, the one property a wrong constant in either direction breaks. clampBoost is the second control, a x0.5-x3 multiplier on the SPAN and not on the radius: the floor stays at R_MIN because a node still has to be clickable and carry its kind code, so x2 doubles the distance between the smallest and largest rather than doubling every circle. It applies to kindRadius too, and is therefore the one size control offered on a metric-free graph, where the channel and curve pickers are not. Its own module for the reason linkDegrees is: nodeEncoding imports stores/quality, and the extensionless-import trap makes that a hard boundary for a bare node --test. Measured on this repo's ui/ scope at File level, 117 nodes on loc: distinct drawn radii 72 at the default, 82 under log, 16 under exponential, and 51/72/91/96 across x0.5 to x3. UI-110 adds the third control on the same module: quantiseFraction snaps t to one of N evenly spaced levels AFTER the curve, which is what automates the boundaries — consecutive groups always differ by span/(N-1) pixels, so the scale is what keeps them apart as the count rises, and where those levels land in value space is the curve's business. Rounding and not flooring, so 0 and 1 survive and the largest node is not stranded alone in the top bin. binEdges reports the midpoints round actually switches at, pushed back through the curve, and is the only thing allowed to say where a boundary is; displayEdges rounds them for print at the fewest decimals that keeps adjacent edges distinct, niceValue's 1/2/5 ladder having printed the band '1,000–1,000' at 8 groups on a 2,520-line domain. Continuous is SIZE_BINS_OFF and the default, grouping being a trade of within-group difference for an answerable 'which group is this in'; below two groups falls back to it, one class being every node at one size."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/sizeCurve.ts", "ui/scripts/size-curve.test.ts"
}

fu f.node_encoding.legend {
    d: "The Legend block in FilterPanel describes the ACTIVE encoding rather than a fixed palette, and owns the size-channel and colour-channel selectors — a reader answering 'what does this circle mean?' is already looking there. legendStops picks sample values by fraction of the domain in RADIUS space, not value space: sqrt scaling had 0.15/0.5 of the maximum both landing in the top half of the range, describing none of the small nodes a real graph is mostly made of. Holding those fractions as radii is also what let the curve become a control — each sample is curve.invert(LEGEND_RADIUS_FRACTIONS[i]), so the dots stay spread across the drawn range whichever of fu shape's curves is selected, and the printed values move with it (50/500 becomes 20/200 under log on this repo, which is where a fifth of the radius range genuinely lands). Stops dedupe, because niceValue rounding collapses two samples onto one number on a small domain. Sample dots are drawn at canvas radii shrunk by ONE shared factor, 22 / sizeLegend.rMax — the ENCODING's top radius and no longer the R_MAX constant, which is to scale only at x1 and would overflow the sidebar at x3; the per-dot Math.min clamp both replaced flattened every stop past 17px into an identical circle, so the legend contradicted the channel it was explaining. The note under the ramp is the curve's own hint rather than the hardcoded 'area scales with the value', that sentence having been true of one of five. The curve select is disabled and not hidden when the channel has no domain to shape, a control that vanishes on a channel switch reading as a bug. Two layouts, because a sample and a class are different claims (UI-110): samples lie along a row, their sizes being the comparison, while groups stack like the severity ramp with the value band beside each dot — eight dots side by side do not fit ~220px of panel, and a class the reader cannot place a node in is not a class. Group rows deliberately do NOT dedupe the way the samples do: a class the canvas draws must appear whatever its band rounds to, or a radius is on screen with nothing in the legend explaining it. Probe legend-dots-to-scale in the ui-014 suite asserts the dots stay distinct and ascending, and passes at x3 where the shrink factor differs."
    cr: "ui/src/components/FilterPanel.svelte", "ui/scripts/ux-probe.mjs"
}

f edge_encoding {
    d: "What a line between two circles means. Colour is the relationship kind (linkStrokeColour off LINK_COLORS), a dash marks the binding, the label carries the kind and the merged count, and thickness is the merged weight — the number of underlying relationships behind one drawn edge, which is the one fact about an edge that node size and fill structurally cannot express, since both already mean a node metric. Which way a line points is not its stored direction: isReversed flips it so the focal node is the subject of a direction-aware label ('inherited by', 'called by'), falling back to kindPriority's parent-to-child order when the selection is off the edge. Edges are drawn under the nodes, so a line runs to a circle's centre and is hidden there — but the arrow head must not be, which is what makes head placement a geometry problem rather than a constant."
    references: f.node_encoding
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/linkGeometry.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte"
    fu aim
}

fu f.edge_encoding.aim {
    d: "Where the arrow head lands, and how big it is. The marker is defined with markerUnits userSpaceOnUse, which is load-bearing: SVG's default is strokeWidth, so the head silently rendered at markerWidth x stroke-width and refX at the same scale. With thickness driven by merged weight and capped, a nominally 6-unit head drew at 36px — larger than R_MAX — and sat 90px back on a 120px link. At Module level, where collapsing merges every cross-directory relationship into one edge, that was the common case rather than the tail, which is why the defect was invisible at file level. ARROW_LEN fixes the head at 10px whatever the line's weight; a directional glyph has nothing more to say for growing. refX cannot carry the setback either, being one constant against a radius the reader chooses over R_MIN..R_MAX, so arrowHeadPoint trims each line's head end to the rim of the node it points at plus ARROW_GAP, clamped by MIN_LINE so overlapping nodes cannot push the head behind the tail. positionLinks is the single writer — one pass with direct attribute writes, since it runs on every tick — and restyleEncoding re-runs it when the size channel changes, or the setbacks describe the radius the circles just left. linkStrokeWidth keeps weight as a channel but re-ranged to 1.2-3.5px: at the old 6px cap it stopped discriminating exactly where it had the most to say."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/linkGeometry.ts", "ui/scripts/link-geometry.test.ts"
}

f grouping {
    d: "Making the parts of a codebase visible without reading labels. The layout answers 'what calls what' by construction, and on a full-repo graph that alone is a hairball; grouping adds 'what lives together' as a second question the picture answers. groupChainOf is the single definition of a group and every group above it, and the force, the outlines and the hover membership (groupMemberIds) all resolve to it, so a highlight can never light a different set from the region it is drawn inside. It delegates to folderKeyOf — the directory holding the file, the same derivation collapseGraph's scopeIdFor uses for Module level — rather than reimplementing it, which is what stops the two drifting; note the vocabulary trap that follows, that MODULE grouping and folder grouping are one thing and not two. It normalises a leading ./ through stripDotSlash, shared with fileKeyOf so both grains inherit the fix, because one analyze run emits both spellings (AN-015): raw, five directories on this repo arrive twice, worst ui/src/stores at 744 entities one way and 85 the other, which is two centroids pulling one folder apart. Grouping may not claim colour or size — both already mean a metric — so it spends position, an outline, and a name."
    references: f.node_encoding
    cr: "ui/src/utils/forceCohesion.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/folderHulls.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte"
    fu cohere
    fu seed
    fu outline
    fu contain
    fu grain
}

fu f.grouping.cohere {
    d: "forceFolderCohesion nudges each node toward its folder's centroid, and toward each ancestor's above that. Two linear passes per tick over (node, tier) pairs — accumulate centroids, then apply — because a per-node centroid recomputation is quadratic and the render budget is 400 nodes. The chain arrives as chainOf, an injected function defaulting to the folder grain — the same seam folderHulls uses for keysOf, and it is what keeps the grain in the caller so the force cannot hold a second opinion about what a group is. tierWeightFor prices each tier at ANCESTOR_DECAY^depth scaled by the share of the graph it EXCLUDES: a directory holding every drawn node has the graph's centroid, so pulling toward it is a disguised forceCenter, and it comes out at exactly zero. The node's own INNERMOST group is exempt from that scaling, being UI-052's promise — which re-bases rather than weakens under f.grouping.grain: at file grain the full-strength pull is the file's and the folder falls to ANCESTOR_DECAY x coverage, a real change to folder cohesion that has to be measured and not assumed. Weights are normalised per node, so COHESION_STRENGTH still means what a reader tuned it to however many tiers a node has, and off is provably 0. A tier of fewer than two members is dropped BEFORE normalising and its share handed to the tiers that can act, which is why a lone file in a folder of its own drifts home to its subtree — and why a file holding one entity needs no special case at file grain, being pulled by its folder at exactly the folder-grain strength. Inert at Module level, where each node already is a folder. Measured nested against flat on this repo's widest scope: kin/stranger distance ratio 0.502 to 0.484 at Low and 0.464 to 0.376 at High."
    cr: "ui/src/utils/forceCohesion.ts"
}

fu f.grouping.seed {
    d: "makeSeeder places every node before the first tick, deterministically, from its path. Wedges are allocated by member count and ordered lexicographically — string order over paths IS depth-first order over the tree, so siblings take neighbouring wedges and a subtree occupies one arc with no tree walk. Angle and radius come from two FNV-1a hashes (hash32) of the node id, sqrt-spread by area; hashing the id rather than the path is what keeps ghosts, which share an empty file_path, off a single point. No Math.random anywhere: one call would void the reproducibility the module exists for. wedgeKeyOf normalises ./ exactly as folderKeyOf does — `.` sorts before every letter, so the two spellings would take wedges on opposite sides of the circle and the force would spend the settle undoing the seed."
    cr: "ui/src/utils/layoutSeed.ts"
}

fu f.grouping.outline {
    d: "computeFolderHulls draws a named region per group: a d3.polygonHull sampled around each member circle rather than through the centres, so the outline clears the nodes it encloses. Self-regulating by two guards that must not be loosened — trimOutliers drops members past 2.5 medians from the median centre, because a convex hull does not stretch for a stray but drags across everything between, and MAX_FOREIGN_SHARE 0.35 drops an outline that is mostly other groups' nodes, which is a lasso round the middle of the graph and not a region. hullDepth draws ancestor tiers too; the tier count limits what is DRAWN, never what a region holds, since a region has to contain every node beneath it or its name promises more than its shape delivers. Ancestry is therefore in the definition of foreign, not in the threshold. A parent (hasChildren) gets outline and name only — three nested washes at 6% stack to 17% and start shifting what the metric fills inside them appear to say. Painted largest-first, which for tiers is outside-in. The name needs one placement pass of its own: separateLabels runs after that sort and lifts any name that lands on one already placed, because tiers make the collision structural rather than unlucky — a parent's hull is sampled from the same ring points as its children's and padded the same, so wherever the child holds the parent's topmost node the two share that vertex and both names are anchored on the same line (measured on the src scope: SRC and SERVER three pixels apart, later a three-deep pile of SRC, PARSER and RUST). It walks innermost-first so the ENCLOSING name is the one that moves, which is both the cheaper move and the right reading — a parent's name is the heading over the regions inside it — and it only ever moves upward, keeping the promise that a name sits outside the shape it names. Extent comes from labelBoxOf, an estimate at CHAR_ADVANCE 0.75 of the font size rather than a real text measurement: the names live inside the zoom transform, so world-space boxes collide exactly when the drawn glyphs do, and over-estimating costs a few pixels of lift where under-estimating costs the collision. The ux-probe check region-names-stay-readable measures the rendered boxes, so a font change that outgrows the estimate surfaces there."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/folderHulls.ts", "ui/src/components/FilterPanel.svelte", "ui/scripts/graph-grouping.test.ts"
}

fu f.grouping.contain {
    d: "Whether a region is a subsystem or a directory someone filed things in — the question an outline raises and could not answer. regionTraffic counts, per region, relationships with both ends inside it against relationships with exactly one, plus drawn against total membership; computeRegionTraffic in GraphView rebuilds it when the drawn set changes and never on a tick, since none of it depends on where the simulation put anything. Membership comes from the same ancestorChainOf the hulls are built from, so the numbers and the outline cannot disagree about who is in a region. Crossing is RELATIVE and that is the point: a link from stores to viewmodels crosses stores and stays inside ui/src, so the enclosing trail says at which level the coupling is contained, and the parent's answer is reachable by pointing at the part of it no child covers. A link with an end in no region — a ghost, an external symbol — is neither, because crossing means this goes elsewhere in the repo and an import of the standard library says nothing about whether a folder is a subsystem; counting those would make every folder that imports anything look leaky. trafficSentence states counts and never a ratio, and never the word cohesion: f.quality_report already reports cohesion as a percentage over every dependency edge in the repo, this is measured over what is DRAWN after the scope and filters and aggregation level, the two legitimately disagree, and a probe check exists whose only job is to fail if a % appears here. membershipText is silent when nothing is hidden — 19, not 19 of 19 — because the comparison earns attention only when a region is filtered rather than sparse. Known limit: a file hidden through the Files filter is pruned upstream of the published dataset, so it leaves both counts and this cannot report it."
    references: f.quality_report
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/regionTraffic.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte", "ui/scripts/region-traffic.test.ts"
}

fu f.grouping.grain {
    d: "Which level of the declared tree is the innermost group. GroupGrain is folder or file — and NOT module, which in mezz already means the directory (collapseGraph's scopeIdFor is dirname(file_path)), so the grain a reader asks for by that name is the one that already ships. File grain exists so f.grouping.contain's inside-against-crossing counts can answer 'is this file cohesive, or a junk drawer', which is the same question one level down and needs no new concept and no new claim about the code — unlike UI-059, which would grow a second grouping SOURCE from detected coupling. groupChainOf is the whole of it: folderKeyOf and ancestorChainOf stay the folder implementation it delegates to, and the file is PREPENDED after the ancestor chain has taken its own limit, so the file is an extra tier rather than one stolen from the folders and the ancestor reach a reader tuned is identical at both grains. A ghost is in no group at either grain, folderKeyOf and fileKeyOf reading the one file_path and being null together. groupGrainFor collapses to folder off Entity level, shaped like cohesionStrengthFor and never overwriting the stored choice: at File level every node already IS a file, so every region would hold one member, fall under MIN_HULL_MEMBERS and silently draw nothing. Landed (UI-103). The wiring was all in GraphView, which had been the only place holding the grain fixed — drawHulls and computeRegionTraffic already took membership through keysOf and forceFolderCohesion already defaulted chainOf — so it now reads currentGrain() once and every consumer resolves through chainFor. The seed changes with the grain: fileWedgeKeyOf gives each file its own wedge, lexicographic order over full paths still being depth-first order over the tree, and a root-level file keeps the reserved arc its folder key had, because a seed that scattered what the force then drags together is the failure layout_seed exists to prevent. A grain change is not an overlay redraw like hullDepth — membership is resolved in the force's initialize, which d3 re-runs only when the node array is set, so applyGrain re-sets it and restarts at alpha 0.5, and it deliberately does not reseed, nodes already settled at folder grain being a good start for file grain. Measured on the widest scope at Entity level, cohesion low: 20 regions against the folder grain's 6, of which 15 are files, bought at cohesionRatio 0.435 to 0.455 and misplacement 0.147 to 0.208 — the re-basing above, still inside UI-102's threshold, and reported rather than gated because Entity level over RENDER_BUDGET draws a ranked subset whose outlined folders vary between runs."
    references: f.aggregation
    cr: "ui/src/utils/forceCohesion.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte", "ui/src/stores/settings.ts", "ui/src/components/FilterPanel.svelte", "ui/src/utils/layoutSeed.ts", "ui/scripts/graph-grouping.test.ts"
}

f viewport_overview {
    d: "Where the viewport is, once the canvas has stopped being able to say. Zooming past ~2x fills the screen with a dozen circles that all look like the middle, and the only ways back were Fit View, which throws the magnification away, and panning blind. The overview panel is a floating corner of the canvas drawing every drawn node unzoomed, with a box around what is on screen. It draws circles and NOTHING else — labels and edges at 176px are a smear that buries the one mark the panel exists for — and takes position, radius and fill from GraphView's live encoding rather than a palette of its own, so it is a picture of this graph and not a second opinion about it. GraphView is the only writer of overviewDots and canvasViewport and the panel the only reader; the command back is a method call (panTo), a command having one recipient. The two publishes are deliberately separate because they change at different rates: the viewport is one object on a zoom or resize, the dots are a render budget's worth rebuilt off a tick handler firing 60 times a second, so they carry the same 100ms throttle as the folder hulls and the same immediate republish on settle, teardown and encoding change. Tree mode gets exactly one publish, from applyDisplayPlan, the simulation being stopped there."
    references: f.node_encoding
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/overviewFrame.ts", "ui/src/stores/overview.ts", "ui/src/components/OverviewPanel.svelte"
    fu steer
}

fu f.viewport_overview.steer {
    d: "The box, and the click that moves it. Everything is a mapping between world (where the simulation puts nodes), screen (world x the d3 zoom transform) and panel pixels; viewportWorldRect is the transform INVERTED, which is the whole trick — d3 says where the world goes on screen and the box needs the opposite reading. overviewWorld fits the union of the dots and the viewport, never the dots alone: panning off the side of the graph is half of getting lost, and fitted to the nodes only the box slides out of the panel at exactly the moment the reader most needs it. One scale on both axes, or the panel is a stretched graph the reader cannot recognise. centreTransform keeps the current k, because the panel moves you and does not re-frame you — a click that also changed the zoom would undo a magnification just set up by hand. Click and drag share one rule (centre on the pointer) rather than the drag picking up a grab offset, which would make a press on empty panel do nothing until the pointer moved; the SVG captures the pointer, since at 176px wide most drags leave it. dotRadius floors at MIN_DOT_R because sub-pixel circles render at an opacity that depends on where they land, making the cloud show density of antialiasing rather than density of code. The panel sits at bottom: 60px, not 12: the mode bar's live-status and endpoint chips own that corner, and covering a status indicator is worse than not having one."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/overviewFrame.ts", "ui/scripts/overview-frame.test.ts"
}

f entity_search {
    d: "The Entities box: type a query, get ranked hits, commit some of them to reshape the canvas. Two phases on purpose — searchTerm drives a preview list and changes nothing, committedSearchIds is the only thing that reaches the display plan. A hit can sit at three distances from the canvas: drawn, loaded but not drawn, and present in the repo but never loaded. searchResults merges all three into ONE ranked sequence rather than a list per tier, because per-group ordering puts a strong match in a lower tier below a weak one in the top tier — which is the whole reason to rank. Every row therefore has to say which tier it is in and what would bring it onto the canvas."
    references: f.visual_scopes
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/searchResults.ts", "ui/src/components/EntitySearchResults.svelte"
    fu rank
    fu classify
    fu reinstate
    fu commit
}

fu f.entity_search.rank {
    d: "scoreSearch parses the query once and hands fuzzyPath's scoreQuery to scoreEntity as a PARAMETER rather than importing it — that is what lets the weighting policy be asserted against a stub scorer, and what keeps the module loadable by the node --test runner. FIELD_WEIGHT is name 1 > qualifiedName 0.9 > filename 0.8 > folder 0.6, the mirror of the scope box's discount in the other direction, because 'graph' means the file there and the function here. Best field wins, never the sum: summing rewards a node for matching in three mediocre places over one that matched the name exactly. matchesSearch is a wrapper over scoreSearch rather than a second body, so the predicate the cross-scope list shares cannot drift from the order the panel renders. Negation is tested against name, qualified_name and file_path together — scoped per field, `graph !test` returned an entity named graph living in a .test.ts file, because the name alone satisfied both terms."
    cr: "ui/src/utils/entityScore.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/filterViewModel.ts"
}

fu f.entity_search.classify {
    d: "Why a hit is not on the canvas, so no row is silently undrawable. classifyBlock walks the same tests as nodePassesFilters in the same order and returns the FIRST that rejects, so the badge names the control actually deciding rather than an arbitrary one of several. It is a leaf taking a FilterSnapshot with type-only imports, which is what keeps it unit-testable. Three reasons are not per-node and are constants: COLLAPSED_BLOCK when the canvas draws above entity level, DISTANCE_BLOCK when a selection's BFS reach excluded it, CEILING_BLOCK when the plan overflowed and every visibility set is empty at once. Collapsed is its own reason and not 'out of scope' — the corpus is rawEntityGraph and visibleNodeIds is computed over the collapsed graphData, so at file level every entity hit reported itself outside a scope it was plainly inside. The two have opposite fixes: out-of-scope wants more data loaded, collapsed wants the same data drawn finer."
    cr: "ui/src/utils/blockReason.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/displayPlan.ts"
}

fu f.entity_search.reinstate {
    d: "unblock maps a reason back to the control that reverses it and records a Relaxation, so relaxedFilters is visible and undoRelaxations puts every widening back newest-first. Nothing is relaxed silently: the reader set those filters deliberately, and a search that quietly widened the view would be worse than the problem it solves. The collapsed case must set autoLevel false BEFORE graphLevel — the level is re-picked from the render budget on the next scope application, and a scope large enough to have been collapsed is collapsed straight back, so the click reads as a no-op. Every other manual level setter in the UI pins it for the same reason. unblockAll dedupes by kind and value, because twenty rows behind one kind toggle is one relaxation and not twenty. CEILING_BLOCK alone returns false: the fix there is to narrow, and widening is what overflowed it."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/searchResults.ts"
}

fu f.entity_search.commit {
    d: "Multi-select over the ranked list. Per-row checkboxes drive committedSearchIds through setCommittedMatches; shift-click applies the clicked box's NEW state to the run between it and the anchor, bound to click rather than change because shiftKey is not on the change event and the box has already flipped itself by then. The range itself is rowsForClick, shared with f.view_search.pick rather than copied, and it degrades an anchor left over from a longer list to a plain click instead of slicing from an index this list no longer has. Committing a blocked row also unblocks it — filtering the graph down to something no filter will draw is exactly the failure the badges exist to prevent. searchHidesNonMatches is the Highlight/Filter mode, drawn as a segmented control that is present before anything is committed, since the mode decides what the search will DO — but the canvas only consults it while committedSearchIds is non-empty, so before a commit the control changes nothing on screen and has to say so, or it reads as broken rather than as waiting. The same applies to selection: a bare column of checkboxes announces neither that it takes a shift-click range nor, when the column is all spacers, that the reason is a scope holding none of these entities. Out-of-scope rows go through one scopeToResults, which unions their files in a single addScopes: one focusScope per row would replace the scope N times, take N timeouts, and leave only the last row's file selected."
    cr: "ui/src/components/EntitySearchResults.svelte", "ui/src/utils/rowPicks.ts"
}

f view_search {
    d: "Search in view — the Ctrl+F over a picture already chosen, as against f.entity_search, which reshapes the picture. displaySearchMatches is derived from visibleNodeIds, so it matches only what the canvas is drawing, and the overlay marks its hits in cyan without writing a filter. That derivation is also the feature's trap and the reason it needs a gesture of its own: a click on a result row sets selectedNode, a selection narrows the canvas to its BFS reach, and the list is computed from what the canvas draws — so for a long time choosing a result rewrote the list it had just been chosen from, and a second choice replaced the first because selectedNode holds one node. The pane's heading said highlight only, no filtering, and its one gesture filtered."
    references: f.entity_search, f.entity_reading
    cr: "ui/src/components/DisplaySearchResults.svelte", "ui/src/viewmodels/displayPlan.ts"
    fu pick
}

fu f.view_search.pick {
    d: "A tick, deliberately not a click: displaySearchPicked narrows the highlight and does nothing else — no filter, no camera, no selection — which is what lets the list hold still while it is being picked from, and is the whole of why multi-select works here at all (UI-101). displaySearchHighlightIds runs the picks through pickedHighlight: no picks marks every match, and once there are picks the marked set is the picks that are STILL matches. That intersection can empty out, since the canvas moves under this list by design, and it is left empty rather than falling back to every match — lighting up the whole result set at the moment the reader's own choice went out of view reads as the tick having been ignored, so the status row reports which case it is in instead. Shift-click ranges go through the same rowsForClick as f.entity_search.commit rather than a second copy, so the two lists in the one panel cannot drift on what shift means. Picks survive an edit to the term, the ids staying meaningful, and are dropped when the box is emptied — picks against a search nobody is running would silently narrow the next one."
    cr: "ui/src/utils/rowPicks.ts", "ui/scripts/row-picks.test.ts"
}

f entity_reading {
    d: "Which entity the side panels are about. Three stores in graph.ts rather than one: hoveredNode follows the pointer, selectedNode is the pinned subject and also the canvas's focus root, hoverLocked freezes the preview so the cursor can leave the canvas. DetailsPanel reads `selectedNode ?? hoveredNode` — selection wins — and renders the hover preview compact, without relationships, which are noise while the cursor is still moving. A hover is only true while the canvas draws the node: mouseout never fires when a re-render destroys the element the pointer was over, so graphData drops a hovered node it no longer contains, and focusNode — clear the hover, then select — is what the panels call, because the pointer is over a panel when they run. A frozen preview survives both, being the one hover the reader asked to keep."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/graph.ts", "ui/src/components/DetailsPanel.svelte"
    fu describe
    fu relate
}

fu f.entity_reading.describe {
    d: "The Description pane reads the graph as prose instead of as topology. buildDescriptionChain climbs parent_id from the subject (MAX_DEPTH 8; Elevator tops out at 4), so an entity reads as its own description followed by the ones that explain what it is for; an ancestor outside the loaded graph still contributes its sidecar description under a labelFromId label rather than truncating the chain at a scope boundary. buildChildEntries is the other direction and one level only — a tree of descriptions is the scope tree with prose attached — rendered under the subject rung as `contains (N)`, capped at CHILD_PREVIEW behind Show all N and collapsed again when the subject changes. It exists because a Feature's meaning is largely the Functionalities declared under it, which a walk that only climbs can never reach. Both directions accept a child whose parent_id is the parent's bare name (Rust impl blocks), but the descent only honours it when no entity actually owns that id, or a namesake adopts another entity's children. describeOnHover off makes the pane selection-only. On — the default — the pane prefers HOVER over selection, which is the exact opposite of the Details column's rule and is the whole reason focusNode exists: the pointer is the newer gesture while it is moving, so a panel-driven selection that does not clear the stale hover first moves one column and leaves the other narrating the entity the reader has just left. The two halves of that asymmetry have to be read together to see it, and being stated in two entities is how a caller came to disobey it — f.spec_pairing.filter's pane click set the selection directly for as long as the split view had existed."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/descriptionChain.ts", "ui/src/stores/description.ts", "ui/src/components/DescriptionPanel.svelte"
}

fu f.entity_reading.relate {
    d: "The Details column's Relationships section: getOutgoing and getIncoming split the subject's edges by direction, sortByOrder keeps the parser's call order where it emitted one, and the incoming side renders incoming_kind ('defined in') rather than the active label. Each row is a button, not a div — it names the entity at the other end, and clicking it makes that entity the subject (focusRelated), which is the navigation the section had looked like it offered since it was written. Both endpoints come out of graphData, so the node is on the canvas by construction: no scope widening, unlike revealSpecEntity behind the Claimed-by rows."
    cr: "ui/src/components/EntityInfo.svelte"
}

f editor_sync {
    d: "The two-way bridge between the editor and the graph, and the loop it exists to not close. Editor to graph: onDidChangeActiveTextEditor, and onDidChangeTextEditorSelection behind a 150ms debounce, push focusFile and focusCursor into the webview while mezz.autoVisualize is on; setCurrentEditorFile runs outside every gate because it only feeds Quality's 'Current file' option and moves nothing on screen. Graph to editor is follow. Both legs ride the same two events, so each side's write is the other's read, and suppressEditorEventsUntil is what keeps a programmatic open from returning as a focusCursor — a timestamp rather than a flag, the echo being asynchronous. The analysis-scope tree follows the active editor through applyFollow on that same event but deliberately outside the panel check, since narrowing the scope tree is useful with no visualizer open; mezz.analysisScopeFollowMode picks file or folder, and the on/off state persists in workspaceState while followSelection does not."
    references: f.entity_reading
    cr: "vscode-extension/src/extension.ts", "vscode-extension/src/panel.ts"
    fu follow
}

fu f.editor_sync.follow {
    d: "'Follow selection to editor': selecting a node opens its file at its line, preserveFocus so the canvas keeps the keyboard. openTextDocument loads from disk, so the file does not have to be open already. isOpenableSelection is the guard and it declines twice. Module nodes, because collapseGraph sets file_path to the directory the entities share and openTextDocument rejects on a directory — the handler is async and nothing observes its rejection, so that was a click that did nothing, reported nothing, and still armed the 800ms suppression window; the window is now dropped on failure and the reason reaches the output channel. And a selection whose span already holds the caret, because cursor-sync turns a click at line 500 into a selection of the function at 480 which arrives straight back here, and following it drags the caret to the declaration — the same round-trip the non-empty-selection test blocks for drag-select, reached by an ordinary click. resolveWorkspacePath tests path.isAbsolute before joining: the analyzer falls back to a raw absolute path for a file outside the analyzed root, and Uri.joinPath appends an absolute segment rather than substituting it. The checkbox is mirrored into ControlsViewProvider and stamped into its markup rather than posted on ready, the view being registered without retainContextWhenHidden — the markup is the rehydration point, and without the mirror, hiding the view redrew it unchecked while following stayed on."
    cr: "vscode-extension/src/extension.ts", "vscode-extension/src/controlsViewProvider.ts"
}

f theming {
    d: "Five palettes in one THEMES array, whose order is both the picker's render order and the product default — first entry wins, currently llvator (amber on near-black, matching llvator.com); midnight sits last. applyTheme writes every palette colour onto :root as a CSS custom property and stamps data-theme, so components style off var(--…) and never a literal. Three things must move together with the default: THEMES[0], loadTheme's fallback, and the :root block in app.css, which paints the first frame before any script runs — a mismatch there is a visible flash of the wrong palette, not a silent one. The chosen id persists raw (not JSON) under localStorage mezz-theme and always beats the default, so changing the default only affects first runs. The graph canvas cannot use CSS vars: canvasChrome re-reads them at render time and GraphView restyles on every activeTheme change. The VS Code webview has no theme code of its own — it serves the same build, so it inherits all of this."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/settings.ts", "ui/src/app.css", "ui/src/components/Settings.svelte", "ui/src/utils/canvasChrome.ts", "ui/src/components/GraphView.svelte"
}

f keyboard_layer {
    d: "Single-letter keys, affordable because the reader can see which ones are live. Lazygit's bargain: every Binding belongs to a Scope — one PaneId or 'global' — and matchBinding resolves the focused pane's set before the global one, so `f` fits the view on the canvas and opens the Filters tab in the sidebar without either being a compromise. keymap.ts is data and matching only, no stores and no DOM, which is what lets the invariants that keep the layer honest be asserted under the bare node --test runner: no key bound twice in one scope, no pane key shadowing a global one (the resolution order would silently take it and the bar could not show that), every pane reachable by its own digit and holding at least one key of its own. keymapActions.ts is the only module that reaches a store, one function per scope rather than one 30-arm switch, and every action is something a control on screen already does — when the two disagree the button is right and this is the bug. runCommand's boolean return is what decides preventDefault, so a key nothing consumed stays the browser's. isTypingTarget is the guard that makes bare letters safe: while a text field has focus only needsModifier bindings resolve, which is why the only modifier spelling in the whole table is mod+k. Two surfaces render the same list rather than describing it — ShortcutBar's chips are buttons, so a key the mouse can also press teaches itself, and ShortcutHelp answers `?` with every scope at once — and displayKeys is where mod becomes ⌘ or Ctrl."
    references: f.entity_reading, f.visual_scopes
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/keymap.ts", "ui/src/viewmodels/keymapActions.ts", "ui/src/components/ShortcutBar.svelte", "ui/src/components/ShortcutHelp.svelte", "ui/scripts/keymap.test.ts"
    fu focus
}

fu f.keyboard_layer.focus {
    d: "Which pane the keyboard is aimed at, and how a folded one still says what it is. focusedPane starts on 'graph' and is never persisted — focus is a property of a session at the keyboard, and restoring it into a window whose panes have been resized or closed since would put the caret somewhere the reader did not leave it. It moves two ways: a digit, and a pointerdown captured at the window against the nearest [data-pane] ancestor, on the press rather than on hover, since the panes are read while the cursor sweeps the canvas. focusPane opens what it focuses, because a pane you cannot see cannot usefully hold the keyboard; 'view' opens the canvas as well, the toolbar being drawn inside that column. The digits run 0 Sidebar, 1 Graph, 2 Spec, 3 View, 4 Details, 5 Description — near enough to layout order to be guessable, with the canvas the one pane not at its screen position: geometry would hand `1` to the spec pane, which a project with no .elv files never draws at all. Collapsing is the mirror and reaches every column: `c` in whichever pane has focus, or Escape, which queues the help overlay ahead of the pane (ui.dismiss) because the overlay covers the pane the collapse would apply to. Escape is global for that reason and not because it means one thing everywhere — a pane-scoped Escape would win the resolution order and leave `?` with no way out. collapseFocusedPane then hands the keyboard to refugeFrom: the canvas while there is one, else the leftmost column still drawn, else where it was, which is recoverable rather than trapped. A folded column leaves a 20px strip, and the strip prints the pane's digit above its arrow — four identical arrows along the edges of a window otherwise answer 'which pane was this' only by being opened. The digits come from PANE_DIGIT, derived from PANES rather than written out beside it: a strip advertising a key that focuses a different pane is exactly what a second hand-kept copy produces, and the help overlay's pane row had been missing the spec pane since that pane shipped for want of the same discipline. `z` (focusExpand) makes focus grow the pane into the window's slack, opt-in because a layout that rearranges itself on every digit press is something to choose rather than discover."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/keymap.ts", "ui/src/App.svelte", "ui/src/viewmodels/keymapActions.ts"
}

f spec_pairing {
    d: "Reading the code through the Elevator spec rather than beside it. The .elv layer draws on its own canvas next to the graph, and selecting in it narrows the graph to the code those entities declare with cr:. One selection, two surfaces — the pane and the Filters pane's Spec section — and the filter depends on neither being visible, which is what lets the pane be collapsed for canvas room without losing it. The filter is a layer over the visual scope and never a write to it, so clearing restores the reader's scope including its exclusions; it is not setScopes, the destructive door showImplementingCode goes through for in-place pairing. Every path comparison runs through refPaths on BOTH sides (normalizeRefPath, pathClaims, pathsClaim, buildPathUniverse) — a ref normalised against a raw file_path matched nothing and reported the whole spec as drift while looking healthy. ADR 0011 supersedes 0005, which rejected this on the grounds that it needed a second GraphView, a second force simulation and a scope store holding two selections; the first two were avoided and the third was simply wrong."
    references: f.visual_scopes, f.entity_search
    cr: "ui/src/utils/refPaths.ts"
    fu filter
    fu drill
    fu mark
    fu pick
}

fu f.spec_pairing.filter {
    d: "crossFilterPaths is claimedPathsForAll over specSelection, and nodePassesFilters hides any code entity no path in it claims. Matching on file_path rather than id is what keeps it working at every aggregation level: collapseGraph mints fresh ids for File and Module nodes, and file_path survives the collapse, so one predicate answers for an entity, its file circle and its module circle. null and [] are different and must not collapse — null is no filter, [] is 'the selected entities declare no code' and empties the canvas on purpose, which is the only way missing cr: coverage ever becomes visible. Not gated on splitViewOpen: collapsing the pane asks for canvas room, not for filtering to stop, and the gate that used to do that made the two wants mutually exclusive. classifyBlock walks the same tests in the same order so a search hit blocked by this reports 'hidden: spec filter' rather than blaming a kind toggle. A spec entity selected while the pane is open is not a selection as far as the code canvas is concerned — otherwise tree mode re-roots on a Feature and force mode restricts to the BFS reach of a node with no code edges, emptying it. The click goes through focusNode and not selectedNode.set, like every other panel-driven selection: a click on a pane is not a click on the canvas, so the hover the graph still holds is stale by construction, and Details reading the selection while Description reads the hover meant one column moved and the other went on narrating the entity the reader had just left."
    cr: "ui/src/stores/crossFilter.ts"
}

fu f.spec_pairing.drill {
    d: "The pane reveals one level at a time: revealedIds draws specRoots plus every open step and its children, so a level exists on screen only once its parent was opened. 8 nodes, then 16, then 18, against 76 flat. The flat version's failure was not node count but equal weight — the Feature you wanted was indistinguishable from the forty you did not, and emphasis had to fade three quarters of the pane to make a selection findable. Roots come from containment rather than a kind test, so a spec using Extensions opens on those, Concepts appear because nothing contains them, and an orphan Feature no Category declares stays reachable. filterSpecGraph carries the claims map over untouched: drilling changes what you can see and never what a selection means, or the same click would filter to different code depending how far you had drilled. trailTo takes the first parent only — a Feature under two Categories has two honest trails, and drawing both is what made the flat view unreadable. A code selection forces its claimant's trail open, so the reverse leg survives progressive disclosure."
    cr: "ui/src/components/SpecGraphView.svelte"
}

fu f.spec_pairing.mark {
    d: "Why clicking an entity would show nothing, when it would. specScopeStates classifies against TWO path universes from buildPathUniverse — the loaded scope's and the whole repo's — into SpecScopeState: in-scope, out-of-scope, unanalyzed, unanchored. Four because they take four different fixes, and against the loaded scope alone a dead path and a narrow scope are the same picture. unanalyzed stops short of claiming drift on purpose: this sees the parsed graph and not the filesystem, so a cr: at a file type outside the analysed languages is indistinguishable from one that died — f recall_bench claims a .py script that .mezz/settings.json does not parse, and the earlier wording badged that correct ref as drift. elevator --drift is the check that separates them. Classification is on the rolled-up claims, not own refs, or every Category reads unanchored, never carrying a cr: of its own. Marking is the default and followAnalysisScope hides instead, because a map that loses rows as you re-scope stops being one. The store this reads is rawEntityGraph, not analysisGraphData: UI-051 renamed the sidebar heading to 'Analysis Scope' and left the store alone, so analysisRules belongs to the VS Code-only tree and defaults to the whole repo — keying off it made the marker inert in the browser, where it was asked for."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/specGraph.ts"
}

fu f.spec_pairing.pick {
    d: "The same filter as a checkbox list, in the Filters pane's Post-Filtering block, and the reason the filter can outlive its pane at all — the fix for 'no visible control' is a second control, not a self-cancelling filter. specOptions returns one list per tier; a checked entity stays offered even when the drill rule would hide it, or unchecking a Category strands its Features checked and unreachable while still filtering the canvas. Drill down is optional here and mandatory in the pane, because the list's whole advantage is reaching a Functionality without knowing which Category it hangs from, and drill-down trades exactly that away. Selection is a union, matching how the kind and language filters read; an intersection would be near-always empty, two Categories sharing code being the exception the cr: duplicate marker exists to flag. A click in the pane replaces the selection because it also moves the view, while a checkbox adds — multi-select is an explicit act on the explicit surface."
    cr: "ui/src/components/SpecFilterSection.svelte"
}

f diff_reading {
    d: "The engine's f.diff, read on the canvas and in the panes. diff.json arrives per entity — a ChangeStatus, a MetricDelta list, and the edges that appeared or disappeared — and every id in it is normalized on the way in, the base side having been analyzed in a throwaway worktree whose temp path is in front of every entity id and matches nothing in data.json. That normalization is the feature's recurring failure: the base-source lookup went in raw while the pane read it normalized, so on any repo whose paths contain one of the markers — src/ among them — every lookup missed, no before-source was ever found, and the pane silently printed the head source as though nothing had changed. An added entity deliberately carries no rel_deltas, every edge of a brand-new function being new by construction and listing them burying the ones that say something; that is right for a LIST and wrong for the canvas, so the added ids are kept separately and the canvas calls every head edge touching one of them appeared — 45 of 86 real changes on the measured repo, otherwise drawn as new code with no wiring at all. The diff can be loaded without filtering anything, which is what the reader wants when the question is what the change sits in rather than what it is. It failed a second time the same way and worse: normalizeEntityId guessed the repo root by trying src/, test_data/, ui/, agents/, docs/ in list order and taking the first found, so an absolute ui/src/... id matched src/ and became src/stores/mirror.ts while its worktree twin stayed ui/src/stores/mirror.ts. A miss is indistinguishable from an entity the diff never mentioned, so every entity under ui/ lost its status colour, its deltas and its before-source in any commit-to-commit comparison, while src/ worked perfectly — which is what kept it invisible for as long as it was. The marker nearest the front of the PATH wins now, not the front of the list, and only at a segment boundary; and the function moved beside normalizeScopePath in diffRollup.ts, re-exported from here, so the one string rule both sides of every diff lookup depend on is finally under a test that needs no browser."
    references: f.diff, f.aggregation
    cr: "ui/src/stores/diff.ts", "ui/src/components/CommitPicker.svelte"
    fu ladder
    fu rollup
    fu verdict
    fu inspect
    fu churn
    fu stash
    fu staged
}

fu f.diff_reading.ladder {
    d: "How wide a diff draws, as three rungs ordered narrowest first so the control says which way it moves the picture: edits (what you edited, changed edges only), rewiring (plus the far ends of those edges), neighbourhood (plus everything one hop away, all edges). It replaced two checkboxes, Changes and Core, which read as near-synonyms, needed three paragraphs of tooltip apiece to tell apart, and said nothing at all about EDGES — both were node filters, and the canvas then drew whatever pre-existing wiring happened to run between the survivors. So the lines on screen were not the lines that changed and the lines that changed were mostly not on screen: measured on a Hybris repo, 37 of 153 drawn edges had actually moved while 128 new ones were invisible because one end was not itself edited. Membership is decided over ids and not nodes, so the ladder is testable without a graph, a store or a browser; which nodes count as edited is f.diff_reading.verdict's call, that question needing the scope rollup and the unknown-file rule. The seed is the ladder's blind spot as well as its input: every rung only ADDS to it, so anything wrongly seeded survives all three and the control reads as having no effect at all. Defaults to edits for the reason coreOnly: true was the old default — on most diffs the ripple outnumbers the real edits and drowns them. Crossing it is a second axis, DiffSeedFacet, which splits that seed into the code that did not exist on the base side and the code that did and changed in place — all, new, existing, defaulting to all, which reproduces the picture exactly as it was before the split existed. Deliberately NOT a fourth rung: the rungs are widths and are totally ordered, the two halves are siblings that contain neither each other nor any rung, and dropping them onto the ladder would cost the one property that makes it legible. splitEdits narrows the seed before planDiffLevel rather than filtering what it drew, which is what makes new plus neighbourhood mean what the new code plugs into — every rung grows from what it is given, so a seed of new entities recruits its context and keeps it, while filtering afterwards would delete that context back out and would be judging the far end of a changed edge, which is context and has no half of its own. The split does not survive the rungs by itself, and that is the trap it was nearly shipped with: rewiring does not only grow from the seed, it scans every changed edge and takes BOTH ends — right, because a swap has identical source and identical metrics and the edge is its only witness — and every edge touching a new entity therefore recruited it straight back. Replayed against a live engine over this repo, existing at rewiring drew 1571 of the 1576 nodes all drew, the control reading as dead above its narrowest rung. So splitEdits hands back what it excluded and planDiffLevel declines to recruit it, which took that 1571 to 89; a swap between two entities in neither half still comes through under both facets, belonging to neither and having nowhere else to be drawn. neighbourhood is left alone: it may still reach the other half as a NEIGHBOUR, which is what that rung means."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/diffLevels.ts", "ui/scripts/diff-levels.test.ts"
}

fu f.diff_reading.rollup {
    d: "What a diff means for a node that is not an entity. Above entity aggregation the canvas draws one node per file or per directory and puts the scope path where an entity id would be, so an entity-keyed lookup misses on every collapsed node — and a miss read as unchanged, which with the narrowest rung and a dim opacity of 0 emptied the canvas the moment a diff was loaded. The rollup answers the question the collapsed canvas is actually asking: did anything inside this scope change. Two properties the rest of the app leans on. Every scope the collapsed canvas can draw is a key, so membership separates UNKNOWN from unchanged — a distinction the entity maps cannot make, and the reason a file created after the diff ran vanished instead of standing out. And a scope is unchanged only when nothing inside it moved, so the rungs mean the same thing at File and Module level that they mean at entity level. A tally rides alongside for the details pane, a file node having no row in the diff at all — compute_diff walks entities, and a file is not one — so without it the pane could say nothing about the very node the canvas is most often drawing."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/diffRollup.ts"
}

fu f.diff_reading.churn {
    d: "How much of the change is at this node, as the size of its circle — the domain behind f.node_encoding's Lines changed channel. The reader could see WHICH nodes a diff touched and never which of them held the work, so twenty circles of identical weight had to be opened one by one. The number is added plus removed, not the loc MetricDelta diff.json already ships: that delta is new minus old, so a body rewritten line for line reports zero and would draw the largest change in the commit at the smallest radius — the same blindness f.diff_reading.inspect records for fan_out when a call is swapped rather than added. buildChurnIndex measures it from the two whole-repo source maps the app already holds, /api/details for the head side and /api/details/base for the base, through the same computeLineDiff and changeCounts the pane renders from, so the circle and the pane can never be two readings of one entity; no engine work and no new endpoint. rowChurn gives an added or removed entity every line of itself and gives a modified one with source_changed false a real zero, that being the fan-in ripple rather than an edit. Both maps are keyed by ChurnSources rather than reached for, which is what keeps the module store-free and testable. The zero-versus-unknown line is the whole discipline of it, held at both grains: an unchanged entity is present with 0 and draws at the bottom of the ramp, while a row whose source could not be read is ABSENT and draws hollow at NO_DATA_RADIUS — and a scope is withheld only when something in it changed and nothing in it could be measured, since withholding a whole folder over one unreadable entity is the worse lie. What is left hollow in practice is ghosts and the sourceless kinds — variable, branch, loop, component — which carry no source for anyone and already draw that way under every other channel. Measured at 8ms over 14 775 rows, once per diff load, so the LCS needs no budget beyond the LCS_BUDGET one entity already inherits."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/diffChurn.ts", "ui/scripts/diff-churn.test.ts"
}

fu f.diff_reading.verdict {
    d: "What the diff says about one drawn node, and — the part that is a rule rather than a lookup — what its SILENCE says. changeOf tries the entity maps on normalizeEntityId(original_id) and falls through to f.diff_reading.rollup's scope map, entity ids and scope paths not colliding because an id carries :line:name. editKind then answers both questions the seed asks at once — is this an edit, and which side of the change is it on — calling a node edited when the diff reports it changed at source level, impact-only fan_in/fan_out movement being ripple rather than an edit, and siding it new when the status is added and existing otherwise. A deletion is existing: it was there to be deleted, and it is the case that makes the control three buttons rather than two, New and Edited implying between them a cover of the seed that a disappearance falls outside of. isEdit is derived from it and not decided again, so f.diff_reading.ladder's seed and the facet that splits it cannot come to disagree about what an edit is. The silent case has no status to read and is counted NEW, which is the reading that matches why the rule exists — under a working head those are the files created since the diff ran — and is the direction whose failure is a node the reader did not expect rather than a node missing from the one view named for new code. unknownVerdict is the third case and it depends on which tree the diff LOOKED at, which is why this moved out of displayPlan.ts and under a test. A → working diff analyzed the very tree the canvas draws, so a file missing from the rollup's key set was created since it ran and is exactly what the reader opened the diff to see: visible. A commit-to-commit diff did not — f.graph_api's diff_handler declines to adopt a commit's head graph, that graph being rooted in a temp worktree the same call already deleted, so the canvas keeps drawing the working tree while diff.json describes two older ones — and there missing means every file added in the N commits since, which grows with N and says nothing about the comparison. Treating those as visible put them in the EDITS SEED, and f.diff_reading.ladder only ever adds to that seed, so no rung could remove them and all three drew the same picture: on this repo at ~120 commits back a comparison of 3 changed functions seeded 2263 nodes, over the 2000 draw ceiling, so the canvas refused to draw at all and the ladder read as broken. diff.json already carried the discriminator — to_ref is the literal string working for a working head and a sha for a commit — so diffHeadIsWorking derives from it and nothing new is asked of the engine. Only the silent case turns on it: everything the diff did report reads identically under both heads, which is the property the test pins. Ghosts carry an empty file_path and dim either way, leaving showGhostNodes in charge of them where it belongs."
    cr: "ui/src/viewmodels/diffVerdict.ts", "ui/scripts/diff-verdict.test.ts"
}

fu f.diff_reading.stash {
    d: "Reading work that was set aside. CommitPicker offered two doors — Current Changes, which is triggerDiff('HEAD','WORKING'), and Compare Commits — and a stash was neither, so the one change in a repository with no way to look at it was the one deliberately put away. Nothing was needed from f.diff, which takes any resolvable ref; what was missing was that nothing LISTED stashes and nothing offered the pairing, and the pairing is where every obvious spelling of this goes wrong. compareStash sends base_hash → hash, never HEAD → stash: a stash's base is the commit it was taken on, its own first parent, and paired against a later HEAD every commit landed since reads as something the stash removed. Both refs are hashes and never the stash@{N} selector, which is a POSITION — one git stash renumbers every entry, so a selector sent to the server names whichever stash happens to sit there when the diff runs rather than the one that was clicked; the selector is shown, and shown only. Its own mode in the modal rather than a row in the commit list, since a stash carries its own base and the From/To inputs the commit mode is built around would be two more ways to get that pairing wrong. The note in the panel is the part no reader can work out for themselves: a stash reads as uncommitted work, so it is natural to expect the canvas to BECOME it, but the head is a commit and f.graph_api's diff_handler adopts only a working-tree head as live state — so diffHeadIsWorking is false, f.diff_reading.verdict reads the diff's silence the commit-to-commit way, and the circles stay the working tree while the colours describe the stash. An empty list says there are no stashes rather than surfacing through diffApiError; nothing stashed is the ordinary state of a repository and not a failure to report."
    cr: "ui/src/components/CommitPicker.svelte", "ui/src/stores/diff.ts"
}

fu f.diff_reading.staged {
    d: "Reading what you are about to commit. Current Changes is triggerDiff('HEAD','WORKING') and shows staged and unstaged work as one pile, so the question a commit is actually the answer to — what is IN this change and what is only on my disk — had no view. f.diff needed one thing it could not get for free the way a stash gave it: the index is the one tree in a repository that no ref resolves to. diff::staged_commit manufactures one — GIT_INDEX_FILE at a copy of the index, git write-tree, git commit-tree -p HEAD — and from there create_worktree and compute_diff are untouched, because everything staged is ALREADY in the object database, git add having written the blob when it ran. The copy is not caution about correctness, the tree being identical either way: write-tree updates the cache-tree extension of whichever index it reads, and mezz only reads the repository it watches. The commit is unreferenced, which is why STAGED is a sentinel to_ref resolved server-side beside WORKING and never a hash the browser holds: a hash would name whatever the index was when it was made, the stash@{N} failure arriving from the other end — there a label outlived the thing, here the thing outlives what it meant. is_working_head stays false for it, so diff_handler declines to adopt the head graph (SRV-019, its root being a temp worktree this call deletes) and diff.json carries the literal staged in to_ref beside working — the same discriminator channel, so f.diff_reading.verdict reads the silence the commit-to-commit way with no new case: the circles stay the working tree while the colours describe the index. GET /api/staged lists the index for the modal before the diff runs, git diff --cached --name-status -z, the -z because a path may hold a newline and a rename is THREE records rather than two — read the source path as the next row's status and every row after the first rename is wrong. Nothing staged is answered there and again by staged_commit returning None when the index tree equals HEAD's, because it is the ordinary state of a repository and an empty overlay would say nothing changed about the wrong thing; it reaches the reader as a declined DiffResponse, which is also what made triggerDiff read success rather than only resp.ok — every declined comparison had been swallowed and then re-served the previous diff."
